{{- /*
Top-level template for the generated entrypoint/main.dang.

The engine loads this program directly and never calls the SDK module to load or
run the module. It implements ModuleEntrypoint: types() returns the module's
typedefs as literals, evaluated in-process with no container; call() builds the
container and dispatches one call to the generated TypeScript dispatcher.

The ModuleEntrypoint interface is not declared here — the dang driver writes
__module_entrypoint.dang into the directory it loads, so declaring it would
collide.
*/ -}}
{{- define "dang_entrypoint" -}}
# Code generated by Dagger. DO NOT EDIT.

# Needs Dagger v1.0.0-beta.18 or later: an entrypoint declaring `sources` gets a
# `types` and `call` with no workspace argument, and an older engine reports the
# argument as missing.

type Entrypoint implements ModuleEntrypoint {
{{ dangSourcesMember }}  """
  Every type this module defines.

  Evaluated in the engine, with no container: these are the same typedefs the
  runtime used to register by chaining API calls from inside a running node
  process. A name-only reference such as `typeDef.withObject("Foo")` binds to the
  full definition in this list, by kind and original name.
  """
  pub types: [TypeDef!]! {
{{- $entries := dangTypeEntries }}
{{- if $entries }}
    [
{{- range $entry := $entries }}
      {{ $entry }},
{{- end }}
    ]
{{- else }}
    []
{{- end }}
  }

  """
  Run one constructor or function and return its JSON result.

  The request goes in on stdin, and the result comes back in a file the
  dispatcher writes, as JSON, which goes to the engine verbatim. Not on stdout:
  that is the function's own, which its logs share.

  A thrown error fails the exec, with exit code 2, so that the exec is not
  cached: a failed call runs again the next time it is called. Exiting 0 would
  cache the exec under the call digest, and every later evaluation of the call
  would replay the error without running the function. The dispatcher reports
  the error in the function error file the engine gives it, and the engine
  fails the exec with it, values and all; raised unchanged, it becomes the
  function's own error, as returnError made it under the builtin runtime. See
  raiseFunctionError for an engine that gives it none.

  The container is built before the exec, outside the rescue, so that a failed
  build, such as a failed install, keeps its own error, values and all: a
  rescued error raised again keeps only its message on an engine before
  dagger/dagger#15161.

  No workspace reaches here: `sources` already put everything this module builds
  from into the module context, which is what the container below mounts and
  what the call's cache key covers. `callDigest` is the engine's identifier for
  this call, passed to the dispatcher so a nested call it makes can be keyed on
  it. `DAGGER_CLIENT_VERSION` declares the engine release the module's bindings
  are generated for. The `dang.toml` beside this program is a separate channel:
  an engine carrying dagger/dagger#14902 takes the entrypoint's view from its
  Dagger version, and the module process uses that view by default.
  """
  pub call(
    receiverType: String!,
    receiverValue: JSON,
    fnName: String!,
    fnArgs: JSON!,
    callDigest: String,
  ): JSON! {
    let checked = requireGenerated
    let request = JSON.encode({{"{{"}}
      receiverType: receiverType,
      receiverValue: receiverValue,
      fnName: fnName,
      fnArgs: fnArgs,
    {{"}}"}})
    let installed = runtime
      .withEnvVariable("DAGGER_MODULE_CALL_DIGEST", callDigest ?? "")
      .withEnvVariable("DAGGER_CLIENT_VERSION", {{ dangClientVersion }})
      .sync
    let result = installed
      .withExec({{ dangDispatchExec }}, stdin: request)
      .file({{ dangResultFile }})
      .contents rescue {
        err: GraphQLError! => raiseFunctionError(err)
      }
    (result :: JSON!)
  }

  """
  Raise the error a failed dispatcher exec reported.

  An engine that gives the dispatcher a file for the function's error puts the
  error the dispatcher wrote there in its exec's error, and fails the call with
  it, values and all: the exec's error is raised as it is. Otherwise, as with an
  older engine or a file the dispatcher couldn't write, the exec error carries
  the end of the dispatcher's stderr, where its last marker line holds the
  function's error; without one, the exec failed before the function returned,
  as when it exited by itself, and its error is raised again.
  """
  let raiseFunctionError(err: GraphQLError!): String! {
    let reported = ((JSON.decode(err.extensions) :: {{"{{"}}functionError: {{"{{"}}message: String, id: String{{"}}"}}{{"}}"}}).functionError != null) rescue false
    if (reported) {
      raise err
    }
    let marker = "::dagger-function-error::"
    let stderr = ((JSON.decode(err.extensions) :: {{"{{"}}stderr: String{{"}}"}}).stderr ?? "") rescue ""
    let lines = stderr.split("\n").filter { line => line.hasPrefix(marker) }
    if (lines.isEmpty) {
      raise err
    }
    raise ((JSON.decode(lines.takeLast(1).join("").trimPrefix(marker)) :: String!) rescue err.message)
  }

  """
  The container a call runs in, minus the exec that runs it.

  Every value here was decided when this file was generated: the image, the
  loader, the package manager, the mount layout. Nothing is re-derived at call
  time, so changing the runtime or the module's layout means re-running
  `dagger generate`. Keeping the exec out of this helper is what lets the whole
  build be shared across calls, and across modules that share a prefix.
  """
  let runtime: Container! {
    {{ dangRuntimeChain }}
  }

  """
  The image every container here starts from, with whatever the JS runtime needs
  before anything module-specific.

  Shared by runtime and dependencies so the pull, the prefix and the package
  cache are one layer for both rather than two chains that happen to agree.
  """
  let base: Container! {
    {{ dangBaseChain }}
  }

  """
  The module's dependencies, installed, as the node_modules a call mounts.

  Built from the module's manifest and lockfile alone — never from its source —
  so editing a function reinstalls nothing. That is also why the install cannot
  live in runtime: every exec after the module is mounted is keyed on
  everything mounted, and anything installed before it under the mount point
  would be hidden by it.
  """
  let dependencies: Directory! {
    {{ dangDependenciesChain }}
  }

  """
  Fail with something actionable when a generated file is missing.

  The engine's builtin TypeScript runtime checks this before it builds its
  container, but under an entrypoint nothing runs that runtime — this program
  builds the container itself, so the check has to live here too. Without it a
  gitignored sdk/ surfaces as a module resolution error from inside a container,
  several layers away from the cause.

  Only call() needs it. types() is literals, so a module whose generated tree is
  missing still lists its functions and then fails at the first real call —
  which is the point: the failure names the file and the fix.
  """
  let requireGenerated: Void {
    {{ dangRequireGeneratedBody }}
  }
}
{{- end -}}
